1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,042
It is the second week of March, twenty twenty five.

2
00:00:03,042 --> 00:00:07,647
Two of the world's leading squid scientists have just come off a ship, and they have failed.

3
00:00:08,766 --> 00:00:12,287
For five weeks they had been lowering a camera into the Southern Ocean.

4
00:00:12,287 --> 00:00:13,912
A rig built for one purpose.

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:19,239
To film a full-grown colossal squid. The heaviest invertebrate on Earth.

6
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:23,370
Nobody had ever done it. Not once in a hundred years.

7
00:00:24,499 --> 00:00:29,097
They didn't do it either. The gear was packed. They had said their goodbyes to everybody.

8
00:00:30,233 --> 00:00:34,909
And then, in a rented flat in southern Argentina, Kat Bolstad opened a file.

9
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:40,160
In her own words. There was a moment. It was a very specific moment.

10
00:00:41,299 --> 00:00:42,658
A low resolution clip.

11
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:49,110
Something pale, drifting at six hundred metres, filmed the day before by somebody else's robot, on a different ocean.

12
00:00:50,233 --> 00:00:51,892
She could not be sure.

13
00:00:51,892 --> 00:00:55,875
In this family of squid, almost everything looks like almost everything else.

14
00:00:56,999 --> 00:01:00,787
So she asked for the high resolution version. And she went looking for hooks.

15
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:05,252
Suckers near the beak. Then hooks midway along the arm.

16
00:01:05,252 --> 00:01:09,900
Then suckers again toward the tips. Nothing else on Earth is built that way.

17
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:13,541
And I was like, yeah, they've got it.

18
00:01:14,666 --> 00:01:18,950
Her colleague Aaron Evans put it more plainly. I started hyperventilating.

19
00:01:20,099 --> 00:01:23,730
After one hundred years of corpses, we finally had one alive.

20
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:27,792
It was thirty centimetres long. It was a baby.

21
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:35,620
And the adult, the four hundred and ninety five kilogram animal this species is famous for, has still never been seen alive by anyone.

22
00:01:36,766 --> 00:01:43,004
This is the story of an animal we named a hundred years ago, from two crowns of arms cut out of a sperm whale's stomach.

23
00:01:43,004 --> 00:01:46,248
And of the one hundred years we spent never seeing a grown one.

24
00:01:47,366 --> 00:01:50,057
Chapter one. What the whale was carrying.

25
00:01:51,199 --> 00:01:55,593
Start with the thing that makes this animal different from every other famous creature in the sea.

26
00:01:55,593 --> 00:01:58,435
We did not find it. We found it inside something else.

27
00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:01,566
The winter of nineteen twenty four.

28
00:02:01,566 --> 00:02:06,567
South Georgia, in the far south Atlantic, is the centre of the world's whaling industry.

29
00:02:07,699 --> 00:02:10,975
Men are cutting open sperm whales on an industrial scale.

30
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:16,215
And inside the stomachs, again and again, they are finding parts of something they cannot name.

31
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:19,762
Two of those parts reach the British Museum in London.

32
00:02:20,899 --> 00:02:22,340
Not tentacles. Not beaks.

33
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:27,743
Two crowns of arms, from two different animals, cut out of a sperm whale's stomach.

34
00:02:28,866 --> 00:02:35,292
A zoologist named Guy Coburn Robson describes them in nineteen twenty five, in the Annals and Magazine of Natural History.

35
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:42,163
He gives it a genus of its own. Mesonychoteuthis. It means, roughly, middle claw squid.

36
00:02:42,163 --> 00:02:45,602
Remember that. It will matter a hundred years later.

37
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:49,580
The species name honours a man called Hamilton.

38
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:58,122
Almost certainly James Erik Hamilton, the Falkland Islands government naturalist, who spent whaling seasons living on Deception Island and shipping specimens back to London.

39
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:02,578
Almost certainly. Robson's own paper is not something we could read.

40
00:03:02,578 --> 00:03:04,987
There is no open scan of it anywhere.

41
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:09,394
We are not even certain where the whale was killed.

42
00:03:09,394 --> 00:03:13,634
One source says fifty miles north of Smith Island, in the South Shetlands.

43
00:03:13,634 --> 00:03:16,895
Another says the Falklands. Those are fifteen hundred kilometres apart.

44
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:20,534
The likeliest answer is boring and human.

45
00:03:20,534 --> 00:03:25,536
In nineteen twenty five the South Shetlands were administered as the Falkland Islands Dependencies.

46
00:03:25,536 --> 00:03:29,109
Somewhere along the line, a filing label became a location.

47
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:33,878
That is the founding record of the largest invertebrate on the planet.

48
00:03:33,878 --> 00:03:38,435
Two sets of arms, an uncertain map reference, and a paper almost nobody has read.

49
00:03:39,566 --> 00:03:42,492
Chapter two. How many have we actually found.

50
00:03:43,633 --> 00:03:48,231
Here is a question that sounds simple. How many colossal squid have humans ever found?

51
00:03:49,366 --> 00:03:54,138
Te Papa, the museum in New Zealand that holds the most famous specimen, says eight adults.

52
00:03:54,138 --> 00:03:56,524
Six of them out of sperm whale stomachs.

53
00:03:57,666 --> 00:04:06,025
A twenty fifteen audit of ocean giants counts twelve complete specimens, half of them juveniles, plus fifty five records that are nothing but a beak.

54
00:04:07,166 --> 00:04:11,211
A review of the species counts about a hundred and fifty five animals caught in nets.

55
00:04:11,211 --> 00:04:14,245
Another paper counts a hundred and eighty eight positions on a map.

56
00:04:15,366 --> 00:04:20,673
Every one of those numbers is correct. They are counting different things. Bodies.

57
00:04:20,673 --> 00:04:21,897
Arm crowns. Beaks.

58
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:24,582
And the beaks multiply fast.

59
00:04:24,582 --> 00:04:30,160
One study of sleeper sharks off the Kerguelen Islands pulled eighty nine beaks out of twenty two sharks.

60
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:32,019
At least forty nine individual squid.

61
00:04:33,166 --> 00:04:39,435
One paper, one shark's stomach at a time, out-counting every intact colossal squid ever recovered.

62
00:04:40,566 --> 00:04:42,562
So the honest answer is a range.

63
00:04:42,562 --> 00:04:50,832
Somewhere between eight and a dozen complete or adult animals, against roughly a hundred and fifty netted juveniles, and hundreds of beaks pulled out of things that ate them.

64
00:04:51,966 --> 00:04:53,595
The largest invertebrate on Earth.

65
00:04:53,595 --> 00:04:58,157
In a hundred years, we have collected fewer whole adults than you have fingers.

66
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:02,068
Chapter three. The ones we never caught.

67
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:07,875
And there is a pattern in the ones we did get, which nobody planned and everybody noticed.

68
00:05:08,999 --> 00:05:14,650
Between nineteen sixty two and nineteen eighty five, research nets brought up thirty three young colossal squid.

69
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:18,638
Their mantles ran from three millimetres to a hundred and forty six.

70
00:05:19,766 --> 00:05:23,332
The smallest whole animal on record is a larva twenty three millimetres long.

71
00:05:23,332 --> 00:05:25,252
You could lose it in your hand.

72
00:05:26,399 --> 00:05:29,638
Nets catch babies. Nets almost never catch adults.

73
00:05:30,766 --> 00:05:36,567
Nearly every large colossal squid we have ever held came up on a fishing line, or came out of a predator's stomach.

74
00:05:36,567 --> 00:05:39,204
Not one was found because somebody went looking for it.

75
00:05:40,333 --> 00:05:43,729
And there is one more thing in the record that is genuinely strange.

76
00:05:44,866 --> 00:05:48,369
Of the large specimens, essentially all of them are female.

77
00:05:48,369 --> 00:05:52,572
A mature male appears exactly once, among partial remains at the Smithsonian.

78
00:05:53,699 --> 00:05:57,760
We have never properly seen a grown male of the heaviest invertebrate on the planet.

79
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:59,655
We do not know where they are.

80
00:06:00,799 --> 00:06:04,038
Chapter four. The squid that shrank six metres.

81
00:06:05,166 --> 00:06:08,949
February, two thousand and seven. The Ross Sea, Antarctica.

82
00:06:08,949 --> 00:06:14,413
A New Zealand longliner called the San Aspiring is fishing for Antarctic toothfish.

83
00:06:15,533 --> 00:06:20,092
Fifteen hundred metres down, something takes hold of a toothfish that is already on the hook.

84
00:06:20,092 --> 00:06:21,802
And it will not let go.

85
00:06:22,933 --> 00:06:25,206
It rides the line all the way to the surface.

86
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:28,003
Nine men. Roughly two hours.

87
00:06:28,003 --> 00:06:32,681
In Antarctic water, they get it aboard with a crane and a cargo net.

88
00:06:33,799 --> 00:06:37,780
It is the first mature colossal squid ever recovered intact.

89
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:42,158
It is, still today, the heaviest cephalopod anyone has ever documented.

90
00:06:43,299 --> 00:06:46,381
At sea, they estimate it at eight to ten metres long.

91
00:06:47,499 --> 00:06:52,290
It is gifted to Te Papa, the national museum in Wellington, and frozen.

92
00:06:52,290 --> 00:06:54,500
It stays frozen for fourteen months.

93
00:06:55,633 --> 00:06:59,513
In April two thousand and eight, they thaw it out and measure it properly.

94
00:06:59,513 --> 00:07:00,622
Four point two metres.

95
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:04,457
The same animal. Six metres shorter.

96
00:07:05,599 --> 00:07:08,397
Nobody lied. Squid tentacles shorten and shrink after death.

97
00:07:08,397 --> 00:07:14,925
Commercial squid tissue can lose up to twenty two percent to dehydration, and this one spent fourteen months in a freezer.

98
00:07:16,066 --> 00:07:23,756
Which means the ten metre monster in the two thousand and seven headlines, and the four metre animal in the case in Wellington, are the same creature.

99
00:07:23,756 --> 00:07:24,895
Both numbers are honest.

100
00:07:26,033 --> 00:07:29,167
This is the first thing to understand about the colossal squid.

101
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:32,302
Almost every number attached to it is softer than it sounds.

102
00:07:33,433 --> 00:07:35,941
Chapter five. Length is a lie.

103
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:39,948
Weight is where this animal is genuinely extraordinary.

104
00:07:39,948 --> 00:07:43,910
Fully thawed, that squid weighed four hundred and ninety five kilograms.

105
00:07:45,033 --> 00:07:46,490
Though even there, be careful.

106
00:07:46,490 --> 00:07:54,359
Te Papa's own pages give about four hundred and seventy kilograms as the estimate for the animal in life, and four hundred and ninety five with ice.

107
00:07:55,499 --> 00:07:58,477
Length, though, is close to useless for a squid.

108
00:07:58,477 --> 00:08:01,455
Look at what happens when you compare two specimens.

109
00:08:02,599 --> 00:08:07,527
An animal recovered in two thousand and three was longer. Five point four metres.

110
00:08:07,527 --> 00:08:09,286
It weighed three hundred kilograms.

111
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:16,546
The two thousand and seven animal was shorter, at four point two metres, and weighed nearly two hundred kilograms more.

112
00:08:17,666 --> 00:08:21,073
Te Papa's phrase for this is that they are incredibly plastic animals.

113
00:08:21,073 --> 00:08:26,182
Total length depends on how far the tentacles happen to be stretched, and what death did to them.

114
00:08:27,299 --> 00:08:30,969
So scientists use mantle length instead. The body, not the arms.

115
00:08:30,969 --> 00:08:35,972
And the largest mantle ever measured on this species is two and a half metres.

116
00:08:37,099 --> 00:08:40,965
Which brings us to the number you have almost certainly heard, and which is wrong.

117
00:08:42,099 --> 00:08:44,621
Twelve to fourteen metres. It is everywhere.

118
00:08:44,621 --> 00:08:48,943
It has never been measured, and it has never been credibly extrapolated.

119
00:08:50,066 --> 00:08:55,352
It appears to be a straight confusion with the giant squid, which is a genuinely longer animal.

120
00:08:55,352 --> 00:08:59,705
And that comparison is worth making properly, because the two get mixed up constantly.

121
00:09:00,833 --> 00:09:03,730
The giant squid has been verified to twelve metres long.

122
00:09:03,730 --> 00:09:10,394
But the heaviest complete one is around two hundred and twenty kilograms, and ninety five percent come in under two hundred and fifty.

123
00:09:11,533 --> 00:09:19,344
The often-quoted nine hundred kilogram giant squid traces back to an estimate from eighteen seventy nine, which modern reviewers judge unreliable.

124
00:09:20,466 --> 00:09:24,024
So. Longest squid, the giant. Heaviest squid, the colossal.

125
00:09:24,024 --> 00:09:29,557
The giant squid is a longer animal. The colossal squid is a bigger one.

126
00:09:30,699 --> 00:09:33,468
Chapter six. The ruler is a beak.

127
00:09:34,599 --> 00:09:37,678
Here is the strangest thing about how we measure this animal.

128
00:09:37,678 --> 00:09:40,477
Most of the time, we are not measuring the animal.

129
00:09:41,599 --> 00:09:45,208
A squid's beak is made of chitin. It does not digest.

130
00:09:45,208 --> 00:09:49,801
When everything else about a colossal squid dissolves inside a whale, the beak survives.

131
00:09:50,933 --> 00:09:54,788
And the colossal squid has the largest beak of any living cephalopod.

132
00:09:54,788 --> 00:09:57,359
Roughly twice the size of a giant squid's.

133
00:09:58,499 --> 00:10:00,073
Beak size scales with body size.

134
00:10:00,073 --> 00:10:05,578
So you can take a black parrot-beak out of a whale's stomach and calculate backwards to an animal nobody ever saw.

135
00:10:06,699 --> 00:10:11,375
The four hundred and ninety five kilogram specimen had a lower beak around forty two millimetres.

136
00:10:12,499 --> 00:10:15,895
The largest beak ever pulled out of a sperm whale measures forty nine.

137
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:22,362
Te Papa's estimate for what that animal might have weighed is six hundred, maybe seven hundred kilograms.

138
00:10:23,499 --> 00:10:31,354
And the museum says, in the same breath, that they cannot say for sure what size of colossal squid a forty nine millimetre beak represents.

139
00:10:31,354 --> 00:10:34,810
There are too few complete animals to build the equation properly.

140
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:38,871
What that beak does not mean is a fourteen metre animal.

141
00:10:38,871 --> 00:10:44,214
The two thousand and seven squid carried a forty two millimetre beak on a two and a half metre body.

142
00:10:45,333 --> 00:10:49,501
So somewhere out there, probably, is a colossal squid heavier than any we have weighed.

143
00:10:49,501 --> 00:10:54,502
It is not a sea monster. It is a slightly bigger version of the one in the case.

144
00:10:55,633 --> 00:10:58,951
Chapter seven. A science built on dead whales.

145
00:11:00,099 --> 00:11:03,887
Which raises an uncomfortable question. Where did all these beaks come from?

146
00:11:05,033 --> 00:11:11,459
In nineteen eighty, a researcher named Malcolm Clarke published an analysis of what sperm whales in the southern hemisphere had been eating.

147
00:11:12,599 --> 00:11:19,025
By his reconstruction, colossal squid beaks were about fourteen percent of the beaks found in Antarctic sperm whale stomachs.

148
00:11:20,166 --> 00:11:27,715
But because the animal is so heavy, that fourteen percent of beaks represents an estimated seventy seven percent of everything those whales had eaten.

149
00:11:28,833 --> 00:11:30,439
Be careful with that figure.

150
00:11:30,439 --> 00:11:34,295
It is a reconstruction from beak sizes, not a weighing of stomachs.

151
00:11:34,295 --> 00:11:38,472
It comes from Antarctic feeding grounds specifically. It is not a global number.

152
00:11:39,599 --> 00:11:42,368
And there is the part that is genuinely hard to sit with.

153
00:11:43,499 --> 00:11:46,331
Clarke published in a journal called Discovery Reports.

154
00:11:46,331 --> 00:11:53,765
Discovery Reports was the publication series of the Discovery Investigations, the Southern Ocean survey that existed to serve the whaling industry.

155
00:11:54,899 --> 00:12:00,620
Almost everything we know about the largest invertebrate on Earth is a by-product of industrially killing sperm whales.

156
00:12:01,766 --> 00:12:05,287
Seventeen different predators are known to eat them.

157
00:12:05,287 --> 00:12:07,487
Penguins, seabirds, fish, marine mammals.

158
00:12:08,633 --> 00:12:14,040
At the Kerguelen Islands, colossal squid remains turned up in around two thirds of the sleeper sharks examined.

159
00:12:15,166 --> 00:12:16,851
Albatrosses carry the remains too.

160
00:12:16,851 --> 00:12:21,232
Though more than sixty percent of what they carry had probably been scavenged.

161
00:12:21,232 --> 00:12:25,275
They are eating dead colossal squid off the surface, not catching them.

162
00:12:26,399 --> 00:12:30,374
One thing to be careful about, because documentaries get it wrong constantly.

163
00:12:30,374 --> 00:12:34,680
Those famous circular scars on sperm whales are not evidence of colossal squid.

164
00:12:35,799 --> 00:12:40,038
Ring scars come from toothed sucker rings, which is giant squid equipment.

165
00:12:40,038 --> 00:12:43,923
The colossal squid leaves a different mark. It leaves hook scratches.

166
00:12:45,066 --> 00:12:48,950
And we do have those, on the bodies of captured toothfish.

167
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:52,127
Bite marks and hook scratches that match this species.

168
00:12:52,127 --> 00:12:56,717
That is real, published, species-matched evidence of a colossal squid attacking something large.

169
00:12:57,866 --> 00:13:00,870
Chapter eight. The thaw, live on camera.

170
00:13:01,999 --> 00:13:06,090
Two thousand and fourteen. The San Aspiring again. The Ross Sea again.

171
00:13:06,090 --> 00:13:09,157
Another colossal squid comes up on a toothfish line.

172
00:13:10,299 --> 00:13:14,641
Female. Around three hundred and fifty kilograms. Three and a half metres.

173
00:13:14,641 --> 00:13:17,535
Recovered whole, except that its tentacles are missing.

174
00:13:18,666 --> 00:13:20,939
And there are eggs inside the mantle.

175
00:13:22,066 --> 00:13:25,855
On the sixteenth of September, they thaw it in front of the world.

176
00:13:25,855 --> 00:13:27,312
Kat Bolstad leads the examination.

177
00:13:27,312 --> 00:13:31,392
It is streamed live on the internet for about three and a half hours.

178
00:13:32,533 --> 00:13:38,664
Te Papa reports more than eight hundred thousand people watching live, from over a hundred and eighty countries.

179
00:13:38,664 --> 00:13:41,049
A museum dissection, with a global audience.

180
00:13:42,166 --> 00:13:47,965
Working alongside her, counting suckers on the arms, is a postgraduate researcher named Aaron Boyd Evans.

181
00:13:49,099 --> 00:13:49,681
Remember him.

182
00:13:49,681 --> 00:13:55,786
Eleven years later, he is one of the two people the world will ask to confirm the footage from the Airbnb.

183
00:13:56,933 --> 00:14:00,094
Chapter nine. The eye that watches for whales.

184
00:14:01,233 --> 00:14:02,177
Now the animal.

185
00:14:02,177 --> 00:14:07,842
And the place to start is the part of it that is genuinely, measurably unlike anything else alive.

186
00:14:08,966 --> 00:14:12,597
The colossal squid has the largest eye of any living animal on Earth.

187
00:14:13,733 --> 00:14:18,984
How large depends on who measured it, and that difference is worth understanding rather than smoothing over.

188
00:14:20,133 --> 00:14:25,697
The figure that survives peer review is about twenty seven centimetres across, with a pupil around nine.

189
00:14:26,833 --> 00:14:29,379
Te Papa measured about thirty on their specimen.

190
00:14:29,379 --> 00:14:33,834
On the two thousand and fourteen animal, they recorded thirty five to thirty seven.

191
00:14:34,966 --> 00:14:37,683
All of those can be true at once.

192
00:14:37,683 --> 00:14:41,758
A squid's eye is a fluid-filled sphere with no bone around it.

193
00:14:41,758 --> 00:14:44,135
After death it flattens, swells and slumps.

194
00:14:44,135 --> 00:14:46,512
Different teams were measuring differently damaged things.

195
00:14:47,633 --> 00:14:51,845
For scale, the largest fish eyes on Earth reach about nine or ten centimetres.

196
00:14:51,845 --> 00:14:55,757
This is roughly three times the diameter of any other living animal's eye.

197
00:14:56,899 --> 00:15:02,444
One caution. You will hear largest eye of any animal ever. That is not safe.

198
00:15:02,444 --> 00:15:09,098
Reconstructions of a Jurassic marine reptile called Temnodontosaurus give eyeballs around twenty six centimetres and possibly over thirty.

199
00:15:10,233 --> 00:15:12,453
So why would an animal grow an eye like that?

200
00:15:12,453 --> 00:15:15,562
And this is where the story stops being settled and starts being an argument.

201
00:15:16,699 --> 00:15:23,308
In twenty twelve, a team led by Dan-Eric Nilsson at Lund University published a hypothesis in Current Biology.

202
00:15:24,433 --> 00:15:26,491
Their argument starts with a problem.

203
00:15:26,491 --> 00:15:30,264
In water, making an eye bigger buys you less and less.

204
00:15:30,264 --> 00:15:32,322
Absorption and scattering eat the gains.

205
00:15:33,466 --> 00:15:40,041
By their numbers, at three hundred metres, growing a one millimetre eye by ten percent buys about six and a half percent more range.

206
00:15:40,041 --> 00:15:44,150
Do the same to a hundred millimetre eye and you gain two and a half.

207
00:15:45,299 --> 00:15:48,003
A squid eye is three times bigger than that.

208
00:15:48,003 --> 00:15:50,706
It is an enormous biological expense for almost nothing.

209
00:15:51,833 --> 00:15:56,169
Unless what you are looking for is not small and nearby, but huge and rare.

210
00:15:56,169 --> 00:16:00,506
Because the volume of water you can monitor grows as the cube of your range.

211
00:16:01,633 --> 00:16:04,492
And here is the part almost every retelling gets wrong.

212
00:16:04,492 --> 00:16:06,779
The squid is not looking for the whale.

213
00:16:07,899 --> 00:16:10,829
Below about six hundred metres there is effectively no daylight.

214
00:16:10,829 --> 00:16:16,101
The only large glowing things down there are animals setting off the plankton as they move through it.

215
00:16:17,233 --> 00:16:22,680
A sperm whale diving hard displaces a cylinder of water and lights itself up.

216
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:24,626
A diffuse glow, whale-shaped, moving.

217
00:16:25,766 --> 00:16:28,927
The squid is looking for the light the whale knocks out of the water.

218
00:16:30,066 --> 00:16:31,478
Nilsson's team modelled it.

219
00:16:31,478 --> 00:16:38,893
At eight hundred metres, in clear water, a whale-sized glowing object should be detectable at nearly a hundred and twenty metres.

220
00:16:38,893 --> 00:16:41,717
Almost twice the range for anything else visible.

221
00:16:42,866 --> 00:16:46,105
And then the detail that makes it sadder and better than the headline version.

222
00:16:47,233 --> 00:16:51,057
A sperm whale's echolocation reaches further than a hundred and twenty metres.

223
00:16:51,057 --> 00:16:55,200
The squid does not win this race. The whale has already heard it.

224
00:16:56,333 --> 00:17:01,740
The most the eye buys, on this hypothesis, is a little warning. Enough time to try to run.

225
00:17:02,866 --> 00:17:05,557
That is the theory. Now the argument.

226
00:17:06,699 --> 00:17:14,092
In twenty thirteen, a group led by Lars Schmitz compared eyes across eighty seven squid species and more than two hundred fish species.

227
00:17:15,233 --> 00:17:18,996
Their conclusion. Relative to body size, these eyes are not exceptional at all.

228
00:17:18,996 --> 00:17:22,469
They are about what you would predict for a squid that big.

229
00:17:23,599 --> 00:17:27,141
On their reading, spotting whales might well be something the eye can do.

230
00:17:27,141 --> 00:17:31,227
But it is a side effect of being enormous, not the reason the eye exists.

231
00:17:32,366 --> 00:17:33,431
Nilsson's team replied.

232
00:17:33,431 --> 00:17:40,882
They accepted Schmitz's revised numbers, called them more appropriately conservative, ran the model again with them, and got the same answer.

233
00:17:41,999 --> 00:17:45,702
Schmitz's group replied to the reply. It has not been settled.

234
00:17:45,702 --> 00:17:50,750
It is, as of today, still an open argument between two groups of serious people.

235
00:17:51,899 --> 00:18:00,158
Which is worth saying out loud, because you will find a hundred videos telling you flatly that this animal evolved giant eyes to see sperm whales coming.

236
00:18:00,158 --> 00:18:01,381
Nobody actually knows that.

237
00:18:02,499 --> 00:18:06,112
There is one more thing on those eyes that almost never gets mentioned.

238
00:18:06,112 --> 00:18:08,612
Each one carries two light organs of its own.

239
00:18:09,733 --> 00:18:14,021
What they are for is genuinely unknown. They might erase the eye's silhouette.

240
00:18:14,021 --> 00:18:15,671
They might be a deception.

241
00:18:15,671 --> 00:18:19,959
They might be headlights, or a way of judging the distance to strike.

242
00:18:19,959 --> 00:18:21,279
Three hypotheses, no test.

243
00:18:22,399 --> 00:18:25,090
Chapter ten. Twenty five hooks that spin.

244
00:18:26,233 --> 00:18:27,147
Now the weapons.

245
00:18:27,147 --> 00:18:31,719
And this is the part that makes this animal genuinely strange within its own family.

246
00:18:32,866 --> 00:18:36,305
The colossal squid belongs to the glass squids. Cranchiidae.

247
00:18:36,305 --> 00:18:42,035
Around twenty species, most of them small, most of them transparent, most of them harmless.

248
00:18:43,166 --> 00:18:44,968
It is the only one with hooks.

249
00:18:46,099 --> 00:18:52,786
And it carries two completely different kinds, which is a distinction worth getting right, because it decides the end of this story.

250
00:18:53,933 --> 00:18:57,196
The two long tentacles are the strike weapon. Around two metres each.

251
00:18:57,196 --> 00:19:03,180
At the end of each is a club, and on that club sit twenty two to twenty five hooks in two rows.

252
00:19:04,299 --> 00:19:08,850
Each hook sits on a short stalk in a shallow depression. And each one rotates.

253
00:19:08,850 --> 00:19:10,367
Three hundred and sixty degrees.

254
00:19:10,367 --> 00:19:13,703
By the museum's own account, up to seven hundred and twenty.

255
00:19:14,833 --> 00:19:17,901
The eight arms are different. Shorter, around a metre.

256
00:19:17,901 --> 00:19:21,991
And their hooks are bigger, fixed, and do not rotate at all.

257
00:19:23,133 --> 00:19:25,211
Most of the arm hooks are three-pointed.

258
00:19:25,211 --> 00:19:29,664
A main claw, and two smaller cusps at the base, to dig in and hold.

259
00:19:30,799 --> 00:19:35,295
The likeliest way it works, and this is inference from anatomy rather than observation.

260
00:19:35,295 --> 00:19:37,222
The tentacles fire. The club hits.

261
00:19:37,222 --> 00:19:43,965
The spinning hooks catch at whatever angle they land, and as the prey struggles they lock from several directions at once.

262
00:19:45,099 --> 00:19:51,447
Then the prey gets drawn back to the arms, where the fixed three-pointed hooks and the serrated suckers hold it still for the beak.

263
00:19:52,566 --> 00:19:55,471
This is grappling gear. It is not chasing gear.

264
00:19:55,471 --> 00:19:58,052
It is mechanical advantage standing in for speed.

265
00:19:59,199 --> 00:20:02,026
And there is something the museum is admirably honest about.

266
00:20:02,026 --> 00:20:08,525
Nobody knows whether the squid controls each of those spinning hooks, or whether they simply swivel on their own once something is caught.

267
00:20:09,666 --> 00:20:11,625
Nobody has ever watched it happen.

268
00:20:12,766 --> 00:20:18,698
For contrast. The giant squid has no hooks at all. It grips with toothed sucker rings.

269
00:20:18,698 --> 00:20:22,405
Two enormous animals, two completely different solutions to holding on.

270
00:20:23,533 --> 00:20:26,302
Chapter eleven. Thirty grams a day.

271
00:20:27,433 --> 00:20:32,846
So you have an animal weighing half a tonne, with the largest eyes on Earth and twenty five rotating hooks.

272
00:20:32,846 --> 00:20:34,199
You would expect a monster.

273
00:20:35,333 --> 00:20:39,617
In twenty ten, Rui Rosa and Brad Seibel modelled its metabolism.

274
00:20:40,766 --> 00:20:45,800
Their estimate for a full grown colossal squid, in water at one and a half degrees.

275
00:20:45,800 --> 00:20:47,688
About forty five kilocalories a day.

276
00:20:48,833 --> 00:20:52,255
Which works out at roughly thirty grams of food. A day.

277
00:20:52,255 --> 00:20:55,677
For an animal that weighs as much as a grand piano.

278
00:20:56,799 --> 00:20:59,725
One five kilogram toothfish would keep it going for months.

279
00:21:00,866 --> 00:21:02,980
Their conclusion, in their own words.

280
00:21:02,980 --> 00:21:06,856
It is not a voracious predator capable of high speed interactions.

281
00:21:06,856 --> 00:21:15,312
It is an ambush, or sit and float, predator that uses the hooks on its arms and tentacles to ensnare prey that unwittingly approach.

282
00:21:16,433 --> 00:21:21,501
And now the caveat that has to be said out loud, because it applies to nearly everything in this film.

283
00:21:22,633 --> 00:21:25,376
Nobody has ever measured a colossal squid's oxygen consumption.

284
00:21:25,376 --> 00:21:28,119
Nobody has ever had a live one to measure.

285
00:21:29,266 --> 00:21:31,434
That figure is a phylogenetic inference.

286
00:21:31,434 --> 00:21:37,939
It is built from measurements of its smaller relatives, scaled up by body size, at Southern Ocean temperatures.

287
00:21:37,939 --> 00:21:42,275
It is the best estimate anyone has. It is not a measurement.

288
00:21:43,399 --> 00:21:46,659
The press turned that study into a lazy, harmless drifter.

289
00:21:46,659 --> 00:21:50,243
And the scientists pushed back on their own result, in print.

290
00:21:51,366 --> 00:21:56,754
Their question. Does an organism need to be an active pursuit predator to be fearsome?

291
00:21:56,754 --> 00:22:02,142
White sharks, leopards, crocodiles and pythons are all ambush predators, many with low energy demands.

292
00:22:02,142 --> 00:22:07,170
It is their capability to hide and strike without warning that makes them dangerous.

293
00:22:08,299 --> 00:22:09,944
And the chemistry backs them.

294
00:22:09,944 --> 00:22:15,535
Analysis of nitrogen isotopes places the colossal squid among the top predators in the entire Southern Ocean.

295
00:22:16,666 --> 00:22:19,216
So not a monster. And not a drifter.

296
00:22:19,216 --> 00:22:26,227
A slow, cold, cheap-to-run ambush animal that happens to sit at the top of one of the largest ecosystems on the planet.

297
00:22:27,366 --> 00:22:30,213
Chapter twelve. It breathes without swimming.

298
00:22:31,333 --> 00:22:35,774
One more piece of engineering, because it explains how something that big does so little.

299
00:22:36,899 --> 00:22:41,088
Glass squids carry an enormous internal chamber filled with a solution of ammonium chloride.

300
00:22:41,088 --> 00:22:46,773
It is about as salty as seawater but lighter, and it cancels out the weight of the animal's tissue.

301
00:22:47,899 --> 00:22:50,799
Every other ammonia-floating squid spreads it through the muscle.

302
00:22:50,799 --> 00:22:53,698
The glass squids keep it in a dedicated tank.

303
00:22:54,833 --> 00:22:58,915
Which means they can hang in the water and breathe without ever contracting the mantle.

304
00:22:58,915 --> 00:23:01,364
They can float and respire without swimming at all.

305
00:23:02,499 --> 00:23:07,248
Whether that is fully true of a four hundred and ninety five kilogram adult, nobody knows.

306
00:23:07,248 --> 00:23:14,073
The review that describes it flags the colossal squid as the family's odd one out, with far more muscle than its transparent cousins.

307
00:23:14,073 --> 00:23:17,337
No one has ever measured the ammonium content of an adult.

308
00:23:18,466 --> 00:23:21,488
And there is a grim little consequence to all that ammonia.

309
00:23:21,488 --> 00:23:26,982
It is part of why the tissue dissolves so fast inside a whale, leaving only the beaks and the hooks.

310
00:23:28,099 --> 00:23:33,977
Which is exactly why our record of the largest invertebrate on Earth is, mostly, a record of its mouthparts.

311
00:23:35,099 --> 00:23:38,181
Chapter thirteen. The dive that wasn't looking.

312
00:23:39,299 --> 00:23:44,288
The ninth of March, twenty twenty five. The South Sandwich Islands, in the south Atlantic.

313
00:23:45,433 --> 00:23:51,351
A research vessel called the Falkor, too, is thirty five days into an expedition named GoSouth.

314
00:23:51,351 --> 00:23:54,680
Chief scientist, Michelle Taylor of the University of Essex.

315
00:23:55,799 --> 00:24:00,352
Their remotely operated vehicle, SuBastian, is descending to work on the seafloor.

316
00:24:00,352 --> 00:24:03,766
It is not hunting squid. It is passing through.

317
00:24:04,899 --> 00:24:09,183
At six hundred metres, something small and transparent drifts through the lights.

318
00:24:10,333 --> 00:24:13,195
And here is the detail I love most in this entire story.

319
00:24:13,195 --> 00:24:15,819
The person who first flagged it was not on the ship.

320
00:24:16,966 --> 00:24:19,690
The Falkor, too, streams its dives publicly.

321
00:24:19,690 --> 00:24:24,359
Somebody watching the live feed from home said, that looks like something.

322
00:24:25,499 --> 00:24:27,861
Taylor's own description of the moment is wonderfully undramatic.

323
00:24:27,861 --> 00:24:37,306
We filmed it because it was beautiful and unusual, and then we kind of descended back all the way down to the seafloor to do the exploration that the rest of that dive was focused on.

324
00:24:38,433 --> 00:24:40,235
They carried on with their day.

325
00:24:40,235 --> 00:24:43,840
And then they said nothing at all, to anybody, for five weeks.

326
00:24:44,966 --> 00:24:48,519
Chapter fourteen. Why they said nothing for five weeks.

327
00:24:49,666 --> 00:24:55,779
Because identifying a small transparent squid is genuinely hard, and because of something that had happened two years earlier.

328
00:24:56,899 --> 00:25:06,695
In January twenty twenty three, a non-profit called Kolossal, founded by Matt Mulrennan, filmed a small glass squid roughly six hundred and eighty miles south of Argentina.

329
00:25:07,833 --> 00:25:12,744
They described it as potentially the first colossal squid ever filmed in its natural environment.

330
00:25:13,866 --> 00:25:17,338
The specialists split three ways. A juvenile colossal squid.

331
00:25:17,338 --> 00:25:21,197
Or an adult of a look-alike species called Galiteuthis glacialis.

332
00:25:21,197 --> 00:25:23,897
Or something not yet described at all.

333
00:25:25,033 --> 00:25:28,429
As of the end of twenty twenty five, it is still unresolved.

334
00:25:29,566 --> 00:25:35,365
So in March twenty twenty five, the team sent their footage to two people, independently, and waited.

335
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:39,252
Kat Bolstad, at Auckland University of Technology.

336
00:25:39,252 --> 00:25:43,578
And Aaron Evans, a specialist in exactly this family of squid.

337
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:48,728
The same Aaron Evans who counted suckers at Te Papa in twenty fourteen.

338
00:25:48,728 --> 00:25:53,685
And the same Aaron Evans who, as a peer reviewer, had declined to confirm Kolossal's footage.

339
00:25:54,833 --> 00:25:56,170
His verdict on that one.

340
00:25:56,170 --> 00:26:00,182
The squid seen here could belong to different life stages of either of those taxa.

341
00:26:00,182 --> 00:26:01,520
He would not call it.

342
00:26:02,666 --> 00:26:07,107
So when the same man looks at the twenty twenty five footage, what changes his mind?

343
00:26:08,233 --> 00:26:10,819
The hooks. But not the ones on the tentacles.

344
00:26:11,966 --> 00:26:16,388
Every glass squid has hooks on the clubs at the end of its two long tentacles.

345
00:26:16,388 --> 00:26:17,217
That proves nothing.

346
00:26:18,366 --> 00:26:19,807
Bolstad's description of the test.

347
00:26:19,807 --> 00:26:28,737
The colossal squid is the only squid that we know of that has suckers near the beak, then hooks midway along the arm, and then suckers again toward the arm tips.

348
00:26:29,866 --> 00:26:34,302
Suckers. Then hooks. Then suckers. In that order, down the middle of the eight arms.

349
00:26:34,302 --> 00:26:38,147
Out of every squid in the ocean, one animal is built that way.

350
00:26:39,266 --> 00:26:45,775
It is there in the name Robson gave it in nineteen twenty five, before anyone had seen a living one.

351
00:26:45,775 --> 00:26:47,077
Mesonychoteuthis. Middle claw squid.

352
00:26:48,200 --> 00:26:51,439
A hundred years later, that middle claw is what confirmed it.

353
00:26:52,566 --> 00:26:55,413
Chapter fifteen. What the footage didn't show.

354
00:26:56,533 --> 00:27:01,522
On the fifteenth of April, they released it. And what people saw was not a monster.

355
00:27:02,666 --> 00:27:09,824
A near-transparent animal about thirty centimetres long, with iridescent eyes and visible internal organs, drifting in the dark.

356
00:27:10,966 --> 00:27:14,734
Scattered across that transparent body were rust-red and brown chromatophores.

357
00:27:14,734 --> 00:27:15,487
Colour cells.

358
00:27:15,487 --> 00:27:20,762
Which means even at this size it can probably switch between transparent and opaque.

359
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:25,371
And for the first time, a colossal squid that was not damaged.

360
00:27:25,371 --> 00:27:29,711
Steve O'Shea, who was not part of the expedition, put it as well as anyone.

361
00:27:30,833 --> 00:27:39,114
It is lovely to see this species being filmed by scientists in a non-invasive way, alive rather than dead on a trawler deck or mangled in a net.

362
00:27:40,233 --> 00:27:41,718
Bolstad's framing was gentler still.

363
00:27:41,718 --> 00:27:49,141
It's exciting to see the first in situ footage of a juvenile colossal, and humbling to think that they have no idea that humans exist.

364
00:27:50,266 --> 00:27:54,190
And she was explicit that she did not want the monster hype.

365
00:27:54,190 --> 00:27:59,748
We get to introduce the live colossal squid to the world as this beautiful, little, delicate animal.

366
00:28:00,866 --> 00:28:03,870
Now the honest part. What that footage did not do.

367
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:08,424
It showed no feeding. No hunting. No mating.

368
00:28:08,424 --> 00:28:12,706
Nothing about lifespan, or growth, or how an adult behaves.

369
00:28:13,833 --> 00:28:17,982
Evans, again. This is not the giant animal we'd expect the colossal squid to be.

370
00:28:17,982 --> 00:28:23,237
We could think of this maybe as a teenager squid. It's still got a lot of growing to do.

371
00:28:24,366 --> 00:28:28,964
Even the thirty centimetre figure is the team's estimate, not an independent measurement.

372
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:33,147
And one thing worth being precise about, because it matters.

373
00:28:33,147 --> 00:28:35,586
There is no peer-reviewed paper on this sighting.

374
00:28:36,733 --> 00:28:40,992
It was an institutional announcement, plus two named independent experts who confirmed it.

375
00:28:40,992 --> 00:28:42,631
That is a strong chain.

376
00:28:42,631 --> 00:28:48,201
It is not the same thing as published research, and it should not be described as such.

377
00:28:49,333 --> 00:28:52,024
Chapter sixteen. Why it stays hidden.

378
00:28:53,166 --> 00:28:57,450
So why, after a century, is a juvenile the best we have managed?

379
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:04,086
Bolstad's answer is not mystical. It is mechanical, and it is slightly humiliating for us.

380
00:29:05,233 --> 00:29:11,722
Much of our scientific and filming gear is noisy and bright, so squid will be aware of our equipment long before we know they're there.

381
00:29:11,722 --> 00:29:13,279
And they will stay well away.

382
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:16,908
They know we are there long before we know they are there.

383
00:29:18,033 --> 00:29:20,279
Think about what that means alongside the eye.

384
00:29:20,279 --> 00:29:31,512
Whether or not it evolved to spot a whale, an animal built to detect a faint moving glow at over a hundred metres in total darkness is an animal that sees a floodlit robot coming from very far away indeed.

385
00:29:32,633 --> 00:29:37,074
We built the brightest thing in the deep ocean and then wondered why the shy animal never turns up.

386
00:29:38,200 --> 00:29:41,282
Chapter seventeen. The hunt is still open.

387
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:44,046
Which brings us to today.

388
00:29:45,166 --> 00:29:48,902
As of right now, there has been no second confirmed sighting.

389
00:29:48,902 --> 00:29:51,619
No adult colossal squid has ever been filmed.

390
00:29:51,619 --> 00:29:55,354
The catalogue of records still ends with that thirty centimetre juvenile.

391
00:29:56,500 --> 00:30:00,941
The Schmidt team is building new camera systems aimed specifically at filming a grown one.

392
00:30:02,066 --> 00:30:06,065
But the Falkor, too, has no Southern Ocean expedition scheduled this year.

393
00:30:06,065 --> 00:30:09,397
Its entire twenty twenty six programme is in the Atlantic.

394
00:30:09,397 --> 00:30:17,060
Press reports at the time suggested it would not return to Antarctica until twenty twenty eight, though no institutional document confirms that date.

395
00:30:18,200 --> 00:30:20,126
Kolossal is still out there.

396
00:30:20,126 --> 00:30:24,750
Mulrennan's line, when the twenty twenty five footage landed, was three words.

397
00:30:24,750 --> 00:30:25,906
The hunt continues.

398
00:30:27,033 --> 00:30:30,351
And there is one last thing, which I did not expect to find.

399
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:36,685
In twenty seventeen, a review of everything known about this species ended with a recommendation.

400
00:30:36,685 --> 00:30:41,871
Baited cameras and remotely operated vehicles could be deployed in the search of live footage.

401
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:46,595
Eight years later, that is exactly what found it. Not a hunt.

402
00:30:46,595 --> 00:30:48,393
Not an expedition looking for it.

403
00:30:48,393 --> 00:30:53,188
A robot on its way somewhere else, and a stranger on the internet watching the feed.

404
00:30:54,333 --> 00:30:57,180
I want to end on the thing that stayed with me while making this.

405
00:30:58,300 --> 00:31:06,894
There is an assumption, relayed in one review from one catalogue, that the Southern Ocean might hold something like ninety million tonnes of colossal squid.

406
00:31:08,033 --> 00:31:10,967
Nobody has ever tested that. There has never been a stock assessment.

407
00:31:10,967 --> 00:31:14,146
It is an assumption, and I am giving it to you as one.

408
00:31:15,266 --> 00:31:19,393
But even if it is wildly wrong. Even if it is out by an order of magnitude.

409
00:31:20,533 --> 00:31:28,882
There is an enormous quantity of a half-tonne predator living in the water beneath our ships, and in a hundred years, we have watched exactly one of them swim.

410
00:31:28,882 --> 00:31:33,777
And it was a baby, and it was thirty centimetres long, and it found us by accident.

411
00:31:34,900 --> 00:31:45,271
Everything else we think we know, we got from corpses in nets, from beaks in the stomachs of animals we were killing anyway, and from careful arguments between people who have never seen the thing they are arguing about.

412
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:49,346
It is not a monster. That much we can say.

413
00:31:49,346 --> 00:31:57,006
It is slow, and cold, and it runs on almost nothing, and it has been sitting near the top of an entire ocean the whole time.

414
00:31:58,133 --> 00:32:02,233
And somewhere down there right now, in the dark, there is a full-grown one.

415
00:32:02,233 --> 00:32:03,697
Nobody has ever seen it.

416
00:32:04,833 --> 00:32:14,220
If a creature nobody has ever watched alive interests you, there is a story on this channel about twenty one people we did watch, and lost, and what actually happened to each of them.

417
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:17,533
Twenty one astronauts who never came home. It is on screen now.

418
00:32:17,533 --> 00:32:20,294
And if you want the next one of these, subscribe.

419
00:32:20,294 --> 00:32:22,779
That is genuinely what decides whether it gets made.

